        { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Hegel would regard Unger\'s philosophy of false necessity with a mixture of sympathy and deep suspicion. The sympathy would stem from their shared conviction that social arrangements are historically produced and therefore historically revisable — Hegel was, after all, the first great philosopher of historicity, the thinker who insisted that every idea, every institution, every form of consciousness is intelligible only within the historical process that generated it. On this point, Unger is genuinely Hegelian, and he knows it. But Hegel would sharply resist the conclusion that Unger draws from historical contingency.' },
        { type: 'paragraph', text: 'For Hegel, history is not merely a sequence of contingent arrangements that could have been otherwise. It is a rational process — the self-development of Geist, or Spirit, through a dialectical logic that necessarily passes through its own negations toward a higher synthesis. The apparent contingency of historical events is, from the perspective of the completed dialectic, revealed as a disguised necessity. What looks like accident from inside history looks like rational development from outside it. Unger\'s insistence on radical openness and the perpetual revisability of all structures would appear to Hegel as a failure to appreciate the deep rationality that history achieves — a kind of permanent adolescence, refusing to recognise what has genuinely been won.' },
        { type: 'paragraph', text: 'More specifically, Hegel would object to Unger\'s hostility to what Hegel calls "ethical life" — the concrete institutional embodiment of freedom in the family, civil society, and the state. For Hegel, genuine freedom is not achieved by perpetually transcending institutions but by finding oneself at home within the right ones. The modern constitutional state, for Hegel, is not a false necessity to be overcome but the actual achievement of freedom in its highest historical form. Unger\'s restless institutionalism — his insistence that every arrangement remain permanently revisable — might strike Hegel as a philosophy that confuses freedom with formlessness, and liberation with the inability to commit.' }
