    bio: 'Cornelius Castoriadis was born in Constantinople in 1922 and raised in Athens, where he became politically active as a young man, joining and then breaking with Trotskyism in his early twenties. He fled to France in 1945 to escape both the Nazi occupation and the threat of persecution by the Greek Communist Party, settling in Paris where he would spend the rest of his life. There he co-founded the influential radical journal Socialisme ou Barbarie, which became one of the most important forums of heterodox left thought in postwar Europe, attracting figures like Claude Lefort and, for a time, Jean-François Lyotard. Castoriadis worked for many years as an economist at the OECD while simultaneously developing his philosophical work, a dual life that gave his thought an unusual combination of theoretical ambition and practical concreteness.\n\nHis major philosophical contribution came with The Imaginary Institution of Society (1975), a work of extraordinary scope that argued for the radical creativity of the social-historical field. Against both structuralism and Marxist determinism, Castoriadis argued that society is not governed by deep laws or functional necessities but is constituted by what he called the "social imaginary" — the creative capacity of a collectivity to posit institutions, meanings, and forms of life that are genuinely new and irreducible to any prior determination. Every society, he argued, lives within a particular "imaginary signification" — a web of meanings, images, and orientations that give its world its particular shape. These significations are not reflections of an underlying reality; they are themselves the primary creative act by which a social world comes into being. This radical constructivism placed Castoriadis far outside the mainstream of both analytic and continental philosophy.\n\nIn his later work, collected in volumes like Crossroads in the Labyrinth and The World in Fragments, Castoriadis extended his analysis to the philosophy of mind, arguing that psyche and society are both constituted by the same irreducible creative capacity — what he called "the radical imaginary." He also became an increasingly passionate defender of the Athenian democratic tradition, arguing that the project of autonomy — collective self-governance — was the deepest and most endangered achievement of Western culture. He died in Paris in 1997, having refused the academic recognition that his work deserved, partly by temperament and partly by philosophical conviction.',
    contributions: 'Castoriadis\'s central contribution is the concept of the "social imaginary" — the thesis that societies are constituted by a radical, irreducible creative capacity that cannot be explained by structural laws or economic determinism. He developed a thoroughgoing philosophy of autonomy, arguing that genuine self-governance requires societies to acknowledge that their own institutions are their own creations and therefore subject to radical transformation.',
    rivals: 'Castoriadis was a fierce critic of both Louis Althusser\'s structural Marxism, which he regarded as a sophisticated form of false necessity, and of Jacques Lacan\'s psychoanalytic theory, against which he defended a more genuinely creative conception of the unconscious. His work also stands in productive tension with Jürgen Habermas, whose communicative rationalism Castoriadis regarded as too procedural to capture the genuinely creative dimension of democratic life.'
